POSNETEK_A = REC(
    "A young firefighter",
    "You will listen to an interview with Emma Walsh, a twenty-four-year-old firefighter from Manchester.",
    """Interviewer: Emma, you're one of the youngest women in your fire station. Was it always your dream to be a firefighter?

Emma: Not at all! At school I wanted to be a vet. But when I was seventeen, there was a fire in the flat below ours. It was late at night, and the smoke came up the stairs so quickly that we couldn't get out. The firefighters rescued us through the window using a ladder. One of them was a woman, and I remember thinking, I want to be like her.

Interviewer: What did you have to do to become a firefighter?

Emma: The selection process is very hard. First there's a written test, then a series of physical tests – you have to carry heavy equipment, climb a ladder thirteen metres high and work in a dark, enclosed space wearing breathing apparatus. Lots of people fail that one because they panic. After that, there's an interview and a medical. Then, if you're accepted, there are fourteen weeks of training.

Interviewer: Was the physical side difficult for you?

Emma: Very. I was quite fit, but I'd never done any weight training. For a year before I applied, I went to the gym five times a week. My brother trained with me and shouted at me when I wanted to stop. I'm very grateful to him now.

Interviewer: What does a typical shift look like?

Emma: There's no typical shift! We work two days and two nights, and then we have four days off. When we're not out on calls, we check our equipment, train and visit schools and homes to give advice about fire safety. People are often surprised that we spend so much time on prevention, but it's the most important part of the job. The best fire is the one that never starts.

Interviewer: What kinds of emergencies do you deal with?

Emma: Fires, of course – in houses, cars, factories. But actually, more than half of our calls are not fires at all. We go to road accidents, floods, people stuck in lifts and, yes, sometimes cats stuck in trees!

Interviewer: Do you ever feel frightened?

Emma: Before every big fire, my heart beats faster. That's normal. But once you're inside, your training takes over and you just concentrate on the job. The worst moments are actually afterwards, when you have time to think about what you've seen.

Interviewer: How do the men at the station treat you?

Emma: Really well. At first, one or two of them doubted that I could do the job, but after my first big fire, they stopped worrying. We're a team, and we depend on each other completely.

Interviewer: What advice would you give to young women thinking about this career?

Emma: Don't let anyone tell you it's a man's job. Get fit, learn as much as you can, and visit your local fire station – they're always happy to talk to people.""")

POSNETEK_B = REC(
    "Insects on the menu",
    "You will listen to a conversation with food scientist Dr Ruth Okoro and chef Daniel Mills about eating insects.",
    """Presenter: Would you eat a grasshopper? My guests today believe that insects could be the food of the future. Ruth, why should we eat insects?

Ruth: For several reasons. First, they're very nutritious. Many insects contain as much protein as meat, as well as vitamins and minerals like iron. Second, they're much better for the environment. To produce one kilogram of beef, you need about eight kilograms of animal feed. For the same amount of crickets, you need less than two. Insects also need much less water and land, and they produce far fewer greenhouse gases.

Presenter: But isn't the idea a bit disgusting?

Ruth: For many Europeans, yes. But about two billion people around the world eat insects regularly, especially in Asia, Africa and Latin America. In Thailand you can buy fried crickets at street markets, and in Mexico grasshoppers are a popular snack. It's really a question of what we're used to. Two hundred years ago, many people in Britain thought that lobster was food for poor people.

Presenter: Daniel, you have a restaurant that serves insects. How do customers react?

Daniel: At first, most of them are nervous. Some people order just to take a photo for social media. But almost everyone is surprised by how good it tastes. Crickets have a nutty flavour, a bit like roasted almonds. Mealworms are quite similar. Our most popular dish is a burger made with cricket flour – you can't see any insects at all, which helps.

Presenter: Where do you get the insects?

Daniel: From an insect farm in the Netherlands. The insects are raised in clean, controlled conditions, a bit like a greenhouse. They're fed on vegetable waste from food factories, which is another environmental advantage. And you'd be amazed how little space you need – a farm the size of a garage can produce several tonnes a year.

Presenter: Are there any risks?

Ruth: Some people who are allergic to shellfish may also be allergic to insects, because they're related. That's why it's important that products are clearly labelled. Apart from that, insects from proper farms are very safe. You shouldn't eat insects you find in the garden, though!

Presenter: Do you think insects will become a normal food in Europe?

Ruth: I think so, but slowly. The rules in the European Union changed this year, which makes it easier for companies to sell insect products. In the beginning, insects will probably be "hidden" in things like pasta, protein bars and crisps.

Daniel: I agree. Children are much more open than adults. When we do cooking workshops at schools, they're always the first to try. In twenty years, a cricket burger might be as ordinary as a fish finger.""")

